4. The ICC profiles
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

`data/icc/sRGB2014.icc`, 3 024 bytes, is the International Color Consortium's own sRGB profile,
shipped unchanged. It is what a PDF/A conversion embeds as the destination profile of an output
intent where the user supplies none (`doc/questions/A18`) and the document's device-dependent
colour is not CMYK alone, and `data/icc/PROVENANCE.md` records
where it came from, its hash, and why this profile rather than one of the other three the ICC
offers — ISO 19005-2 section 6.2.4.2 names the ICC texts a profile may conform to, and the
obvious v4 download is not one of them.

The ICC's profile library states, for profiles it holds the copyright in:

      This profile is made available by the International Color Consortium, and may be copied,
      distributed, embedded, made, used, and sold without restriction. Altered versions of this
      profile shall have the original identification and copyright information removed and shall
      not be misrepresented as the original profile.

It is shipped unaltered, with its `cprt` tag — "Copyright International Color Consortium, 2015" —
intact, which also satisfies the narrower grant the ICC attaches to its v4 profiles.

`data/icc/GRACoL2006_Coated1v2.icc`, 2 747 956 bytes, is the CMYK profile beside it, taken from the
same registry and shipped unchanged. A conversion embeds it where the document's device-dependent
colour is CMYK and the user supplied no profile, on the same clause and the same reading
(`doc/adr/1153`). Its own `cprt` tag is its licence:

      Copyright X-Rite, Inc.. This profile is made available by IDEAlliance, with permission of
      X-Rite, Inc., and may be used, embedded, exchanged, and shared without restriction.  It may
      not be altered, or sold without written permission of IDEAlliance.

It is shipped unaltered, which is the one thing that grant forbids, and
`data/icc/PROVENANCE.md` records its hash, its tags, and the profiles declined beside it.


